In C++11, you can use the new function template syntax to create a variadic template function:

```cpp
template <typename... Args>
void f(Args... args) {
    // use args...
}
```

This function can take any number and type of arguments. The `Args...` part is a pack expansion, which expands into a list of the types of the arguments passed to the function. For example, if you call `f(1, "two", 3.0f)`, `Args...` will expand to `int, const char[3], float`. You can then use these types in your function body. For example, to print the types of the arguments:

```cpp
template <typename... Args>
void f(Args... args) {
    (args + ...).dump(); // prints types of all arguments
}
```

This uses the `...` operator to replicate the `args` pack and pass it to `std::cout << ...`, which prints the types of all arguments.

