```cpp
#include <iostream>

template<typename... Args>
void print(Args... args) {
    (args...);
    std::cout << "Arguments: ";
    for (auto arg : {args...}) {
        std::cout << arg << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    print("Hello", 42, 3.14);
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `Args...` is a parameter pack that captures all the arguments passed to the `print` function. The `(args...)` syntax is used to expand the parameter pack, allowing us to use its elements in a variadic context.

