The discrepancy between the FILETIME conversion and the Windows file properties date arises from the way Windows handles file timestamps. 

FILETIME represents the number of 100-nanosecond intervals since January 1, 1601 (UTC). When converting FILETIME to a DateTime object, the conversion assumes UTC time. However, the date displayed in Windows file properties reflects the local time zone of the system where the file resides.

To align the FILETIME conversion with the Windows file properties date, you need to account for the local time zone offset. Here's an adjusted method to convert FILETIME to a DateTime object considering the local time zone:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Globalization;

public static DateTime ConvertToDateTimeWithTimeZoneOffset(System.Runtime.InteropServices.ComTypes.FILETIME time, TimeZoneInfo timeZoneInfo)
{
    long highBits = time.dwHighDateTime;
    highBits = highBits << 32;
    long fileTime = highBits + time.dwLowDateTime;

    // Convert FILETIME to UTC DateTime
    DateTime utcDateTime = DateTime.FromFileTimeUtc(fileTime);

    // Adjust for the specified time zone offset
    utcDateTime = utcDateTime.AddHours(timeZoneInfo.GetUtcOffset(utcDateTime).Hours);

