Extension methods must be static because they are not instance methods. They are static methods that are called as if they were instance methods on the type. This is why you can't access instance members from within an extension method.

The signature of an extension method must be:

public static ResultName ReturnType(ClassName this, Argument1 arg1, Argument2 arg2, ...)

The ClassName is the type you want to extend. The this keyword indicates that the method is an extension method. The arguments are the parameters of the method.

The return type of an extension method must be explicit. You can't use var.

The extension method is called as if it was an instance method on the ClassName:

ClassName instance = ...;
Result result = instance.MethodName(arg1, arg2, ...);

This is why you can't access instance members from within an extension method. The this keyword in the method signature refers to the instance on which the method is called, not the instance of the class that defines the method.

In your case, you want to extend IQueryable<T> to add ordering capabilities. The method signature should look like this:

