@synthesize window;

instead of

@synthesize window=_window;

in Objective-C?

In Objective-C, the @synthesize directive is used to automatically generate getter and setter methods for an instance variable. When you use @synthesize without specifying a variable name, the compiler will automatically generate a variable with the same name as the property.

For example:

@property (nonatomic, strong) NSString *title;

If you use @synthesize without specifying a variable name, the compiler will generate an instance variable named "title".

However, if you want to use a different name for the instance variable, you can specify it in the @synthesize directive:

@synthesize title = _title;

In this case, the compiler will generate an instance variable named "_title" instead of "title".

The main difference between using a single underscore and a double underscore is a matter of convention and readability:

- A single underscore is often used as a prefix to indicate that an instance variable is private and should not be accessed directly outside of the class implementation.
- A double underscore is used to indicate that the instance variable is a synthesized variable generated by the @synthesize directive.

