The issue is that Dagger 2 does not support generic types in the injection targets. The @Inject annotation can only be used on fields or methods that are not generic.

The solution is to create a non-generic interface that your generic type implements, and then inject that interface.

Here's an example:

public interface ServiceInterface<T> {
    void doSomething(T t);
}

public class Service<T> implements ServiceInterface<T> {
    @Override
    public void doSomething(T t) {
        // do something with t
    }
}

Then, in your Dagger module, you can provide the ServiceInterface:

@Module
public class ServiceModule {
    @Provides @Singleton ServiceInterface<Project> provideServiceInterface() {
        return new Service<Project>();
    }
}

And in your BaseListFragment, you can inject the ServiceInterface:

public abstract class BaseListFragment<E, A extends ArrayAdapter<E>, S> extends BaseFragment {
    @Inject ServiceInterface<S> service;
}

This way, Dagger 2 can inject the ServiceInterface, and you can cast it to the appropriate Service<T> type within your fragment.

