The solution to exit a chroot environment within a Perl script is to utilize the `open` function to redirect the standard input, output, and error streams back to the original filesystem. Here's how you can achieve this:

```perl
# Mount the disk image and chroot into it
mount $disk_image $mount_point;
chdir $mount_point;
chroot $mount_point;

# Do your things...

# Redirect standard I/O back to the original filesystem
open STDIN,  "<&",  fileno(*STDIN);
open STDOUT, ">&", fileno(*STDOUT);
open STDERR, ">&", fileno(*STDSTDERR);

# Now you can execute commands or scripts that rely on the original filesystem
# ...

# Exit the chroot environment
exit;
