In React, when you want to extend the properties of a standard HTML element, you can use the `DetailedHTMLProps` utility. This allows you to specify additional HTML attributes directly on your component's props. On the other hand, `HTMLAttributes` is a generic type that represents the attributes commonly used in HTML elements. It includes a subset of HTML attributes that are frequently used, such as `className`, `style`, `key`, and others.

When you want to extend the properties of a standard `<div>` element, you can define your component's interface to accept `DetailedHTMLProps<React.HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>, HTMLElement>`. This means your component can receive any HTML attributes applicable to a `div` element, plus additional custom attributes you might need.

For example, if you have a component `MyCustomDiv`, its interface could look like this:

```typescript
interface MyCustomDivProps extends DetailedHTMLProps<React.HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>, HTMLElement> {
  extraProp: string;
}

const MyCustomDiv: React.FC<MyCustomDivProps> = ({ extraProp, ...otherProps }) => {
