The setInterval function in JavaScript is designed to execute a function at regular intervals, but it does not guarantee exact timing. This is due to the single-threaded nature of JavaScript execution in the browser, where the function call may be delayed by other tasks such as user interactions or rendering.

To achieve more precise timing, you can use the setTimeout function in combination with a recursive call to your function. This approach allows you to dynamically adjust the delay based on the actual execution time of each call. Here's an example:

```javascript
var start = null;
var nextAt = null;

function f() {
    if (!start) {
        start = Date.now(); // Using Date.now() for better precision
        nextAt = start + 1000; // 1000 milliseconds
    }

    var now = Date.now();
    var drift = (now - start) % 1000; // Calculate the drift in milliseconds

    // Append the drift to the DOM for visualization
    document.querySelector('#results').innerHTML += `${drift}ms `;

    // Schedule the next call
    setTimeout(f, nextAt - now);
}

// Initialize the first call
setTimeout(f, 1000); // Initial delay of 1000 milliseconds
