The reason JSLint and many JavaScript style guides recommend using `{}` immediately after `else` is to avoid potential issues with automatic semicolon insertion (ASI). 

ASI is a feature in JavaScript that automatically inserts semicolons at the end of certain lines to ensure the code remains syntactically correct. However, this feature can sometimes lead to unexpected behavior, especially when dealing with line breaks and certain keywords like `else`.

Consider the following example:

```javascript
if (condition) {
    statement1;
    statement2;
} else
    statement3; // This line is treated as `statement3;` instead of `statement3` being part of the `else` block

```

In this case, because `else` is on the same line as the closing brace `}`, ASI treats the following line as a separate statement, leading to an error or unexpected behavior.

By using `{}` immediately after `else`, you ensure that the `else` block is clearly delineated and ASI does not mistakenly treat the following line as a separate statement:

```javascript
if (condition) {
    statement1;
    statement2;
} else {
    statement3;
}
```

This practice helps maintain consistency and reduces the risk of errors due to ASI

